    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 74,
      "topLeftY": 228,
      "bottomRightX": 264,
      "bottomRightY": 243,
      "content": "From (26a) and (17) we have however",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 288,
      "topLeftY": 240,
      "bottomRightX": 430,
      "bottomRightY": 274,
      "content": "$$\\left(H_{0}^{(1)}\\right)_{nn} = V_{n}^{(1)} = \\sum_{i} \\zeta_{i} \\frac{\\partial V_{n}}{\\partial \\xi_{i}}.$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 74,
      "topLeftY": 278,
      "bottomRightX": 107,
      "bottomRightY": 291,
      "content": "Thus:",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 335,
      "topLeftY": 287,
      "bottomRightX": 643,
      "bottomRightY": 318,
      "content": "$$\\frac{\\partial V_{n}}{\\partial \\xi_{i}} = 0. \\tag{40}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 74,
      "topLeftY": 321,
      "bottomRightX": 644,
      "bottomRightY": 387,
      "content": "The validity of continuing our approximation procedure requires that the relative nuclear coordinates $\\xi_{i}$ must not be arbitrarily chosen, but must correspond to an extremum of the electronic energy $V_{n}(\\xi)$. The existence of this is therefore the condition for the existence of the molecule, a law which is usually assumed to be self-evident. We will show later that it must necessarily be a minimum as well.",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 74,
      "topLeftY": 388,
      "bottomRightX": 643,
      "bottomRightY": 424,
      "content": "The function $\\chi_{n}^{(0)}(\\zeta,\\theta)$ remains, as yet, undetermined. Setting in (37) $W_{n}^{(0)} = V_{n}(\\xi)$, $W_{n}^{(1)} = 0$ and $\\psi_{n}^{(0)} = \\chi_{n}^{(0)}\\phi_{n}^{(0)}$ we find the equation which determines $\\phi_{n}^{(1)}$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 276,
      "topLeftY": 431,
      "bottomRightX": 643,
      "bottomRightY": 452,
      "content": "$$\\left(H_{0}^{(0)} - V_{n}^{(0)}\\right)\\psi_{n}^{(1)} = -H_{0}^{(1)}\\phi_{n}^{(0)}\\chi_{n}^{(0)}. \\tag{41}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 74,
      "topLeftY": 459,
      "bottomRightX": 644,
      "bottomRightY": 494,
      "content": "A solution of this by (25a) is $\\psi_{n}^{(1)} = \\chi_{n}^{(0)}\\phi_{n}^{(1)}$ where $\\phi_{n}^{(1)}$ is the function (19a) defined by (18). The general solution is obtained by adding a solution $\\phi_{n}^{(0)}$ of the homogeneous equation with the yet undetermined factor $\\chi_{n}^{(1)}(\\xi,\\theta)$:",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 297,
      "topLeftY": 503,
      "bottomRightX": 643,
      "bottomRightY": 523,
      "content": "$$\\psi_{n}^{(1)} = \\chi_{n}^{(0)}\\phi_{n}^{(1)} + \\chi_{n}^{(1)}\\phi_{n}^{(0)}. \\tag{42}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 74,
      "topLeftY": 542,
      "bottomRightX": 554,
      "bottomRightY": 557,
      "content": "### Part V. Solution of the Approximate Equations of second and third Order:Nuclear Vibration",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "title"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 74,
      "topLeftY": 570,
      "bottomRightX": 644,
      "bottomRightY": 601,
      "content": "We now reach the approximation equation (35c), which after substitution of the solutions for the lower order approximations is",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 233,
      "topLeftY": 609,
      "bottomRightX": 643,
      "bottomRightY": 654,
      "content": "$$\\begin{array}{l} \\left(H_{0}^{(0)} - V_{n}^{(0)}\\right)\\psi_{n}^{(2)} = \\left(W_{n}^{(2)} - H_{0}^{(2)} - H_{\\zeta\\zeta}^{(0)}\\right)\\chi_{n}^{(0)}\\phi_{n}^{(0)} \\\\ \\quad - H_{0}^{(1)}\\left(\\chi_{n}^{(0)}\\phi_{n}^{(1)} + \\chi_{n}^{(1)}\\phi_{n}^{(0)}\\right). \\tag{43} \\end{array}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 74,
      "topLeftY": 661,
      "bottomRightX": 643,
      "bottomRightY": 694,
      "content": "In order for this to be solvable, the right-hand side must again be orthogonal to $\\phi_{n}^{(0)}$; using the notation of part II this yields, because of (39):",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 252,
      "topLeftY": 694,
      "bottomRightX": 463,
      "bottomRightY": 717,
      "content": "$$\\left\\{\\left(H_{0}^{(2)} + H_{\\zeta\\zeta}^{(0)}\\right)_{nn} + \\left(H_{0}^{(1)}\\right)_{nn} - W_{n}^{(2)}\\right\\}\\chi_{n}^{(0)} = 0.$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 74,
      "topLeftY": 719,
      "bottomRightX": 269,
      "bottomRightY": 736,
      "content": "It follows from (26b) with $V_{n}^{(1)} = 0$ that",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 293,
      "topLeftY": 734,
      "bottomRightX": 643,
      "bottomRightY": 758,
      "content": "$$\\left(H_{0}^{(2)}\\right)_{nn} + \\left(H_{0}^{(1)}\\right)_{nn}^{(1)} = V_{n}^{(2)}. \\tag{44}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 74,
      "topLeftY": 761,
      "bottomRightX": 384,
      "bottomRightY": 782,
      "content": "Since $H_{\\zeta\\zeta}^{(0)}$ by (30a) is seen to be independent of the $x_{k}$ we find:",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 287,
      "topLeftY": 789,
      "bottomRightX": 643,
      "bottomRightY": 810,
      "content": "$$\\left\\{H_{\\xi\\xi}^{(0)} + V_{n}^{(2)} - W_{n}^{(2)}\\right\\}\\chi_{n}^{(0)} = 0. \\tag{45}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 74,
      "topLeftY": 817,
      "bottomRightX": 643,
      "bottomRightY": 848,
      "content": "Noting the meanings of $H_{\\zeta\\zeta}^{(0)}$ and $V_{n}^{(2)}$ given by (17c) and (30a) we see that (45) represents the equation for harmonic nuclear vibration:",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
